# Firsthand checks

Two habits: look for what is already known before building anything, and give every
claim that will steer a decision or be written down one cheap check of your own first.
Each rule: the rule, why, and when it does not apply.

## Consult before constructing{{#if modules.attempts}} (principle: consult-before-constructing){{/if}}

Before building a non-trivial method (an algorithm, a proof strategy, a search, a
sampling scheme), look for an existing treatment. The search has two halves, and both are
standing practice: neither needs permission.

**Internal half.** Search the project's own record first:
{{#if modules.conjectures}}
the conjecture files in `wiki/conjectures/` (including their `@residue` lines, whatever
their status),
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.questions}}
every conjecture attached to the same research question (`bin/question-check --tsv`), not
only the one you are working on, since a sibling has often settled the point already,
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.known_results}}
the catalog of published results in `wiki/known-results/`,
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.experiments}}
earlier experiments (`experiments/_index.md`),
{{/if}}
the notes and sources under `wiki/`, and `notes/`. Say what you found, or "nothing
found", before you start.
- Why: re-deriving the project's own result, and then reporting it as new, is the most
  common waste, and the cheapest to prevent.
- If a relevant source has been collected but not yet summarized, summarize it first;
  do not experiment straight from unread material.

**External half.**{{#if modules.attempts}} (principle: lit-search-before-claim){{/if}} Send a literature search to the `lit-search` agent whenever a finding is
being characterized, a mechanism named, a conjecture promoted, or a claim reaches "is this
already known?". Do it at the moment you are about to design a durable edit that rests on
such a claim, not only at commit time. Scope the search to what the internal half did not
cover; put a line starting `Internal prior art:` in the brief saying what that was.
- Why: a few minutes of searching is cheap insurance against re-proving a known theorem
  and against naming a mechanism after the wrong source.
- **Before the first attempt on a hard target**, run the literature pass as its own dispatch,
  and hand the attempting agent what it found as facts, conventions and named techniques
  (the brief's `Known` line), not as "this is a famous open problem". When a result comes
  back, ask whether it reuses a known construction or theorem without saying so: models
  reproduce what they have read without attribution.
- **When a structure emerges** in a computation or a proof (a closed form, a recurrence, an
  algebraic or combinatorial pattern), ask what it is known as before building on it: list
  candidate names, search under each, and check a fresh derivation for being a known
  construction. A name, once found, brings the literature that goes with it.
- Hooks: `lit-search-predispatch` {{#if harness.cap.spawn_brief_visible}}refuses the first search you dispatch in a
  session, once, with a reminder of the internal half; the identical retry goes through{{else}}records each search (this harness hides the brief from
  hooks, so the `lit-search` agent itself checks for the `Internal prior art:` line){{/if}}; `lit-search-gate` nudges a commit that adds a status
  change or an attribution to a durable document when no search has been recorded since
  the file was last committed (or in the past 24 hours, for a new file), whatever the
  search was about.

## One firsthand check per load-bearing claim

A claim is load-bearing when it decides what work happens (build or reuse, continue or
stop) or when it is about to enter a durable record (a commit, a ledger, a report, a
handoff). Every such claim gets one cheap check of your own before it propagates, whoever
made it. One check means one command, one probe or one read, not a research project.
Routine exploration ("I didn't find it, I'll write the small lemma myself") is exempt: it
decides nothing durable. A hedge such as "citation unverified" limits the damage of a
wrong claim but does not remove the cost of correcting it later; prefer the check.

**A citation is a reading, not a fact.** When a search agent (or anyone) says "this is
Theorem 3.2 of X" or "this is a corollary of Y", that is its reading of a source.
Placement claims ("this is open", "X studied this", "the best known bound is…") are lower
risk; act on them, except when one would start or stop a line of work: then it is
load-bearing and gets its one check (open the paper said to settle it, or the most recent
paper or survey said to state it as open, and confirm). Before a theorem number or a derivation goes into a durable document,
open the source and read the statement yourself, together with the source's definitions
of its terms and any standing hypotheses of that section, or keep it in one draft, marked
"citation unverified". When the source is out of reach (a textbook with no copy at hand),
two independent restatements of the theorem with its hypotheses are a corroborated
reading{{#if modules.known_results}} (`docs/discipline/known-results.md`){{/if}}; the citation stays the original source.
If neither is to be had, list the citation for {{owner}} in your report ("Theorem X of Y,
used in Z: can you check it?"): they may have the book.
- Why: a wrong citation spreads to every document that copies it, and the classic error
  is a correct theorem about a neighbouring object (a different family, a different
  hypothesis, a different notion of the same word).

**An absence claim needs a whole-library name search.** "The library has no X", "no module
here provides X" all decide whether you build something.
Before recording one, run one search over the whole tree by name, never by guessed
location:
{{#if modules.lean}}
`bin/lib-find <name-regex>` for the proof library, and a project-wide `grep` for your
own code.
{{else}}
search the library by declaration name across the whole tree, and `grep` the whole
project for your own code.
{{/if}}
A search of the directory where you expected to find it is not a search.
- Why: an over-pessimistic "it doesn't exist" kills a reuse as surely as a wrong citation
  spreads an error, and the same name often lives somewhere unexpected.
- Also for absences you derived yourself this session: a new instance you have not
  checked against the standing hypotheses, a self-diagnosed "type-class problem" you have
  not actually reproduced. A correct headline does not vouch for its details; check each
  detail that carries weight.

**A negative scope verdict needs the source read and one scout.** Before reporting that
something is too hard, out of scope, a multi-week effort, or blocked on theory that does
not exist, read the source's own statement and proof structure (not your paraphrase of
the goal), and run one search of the libraries and literature for the missing piece.
- Why: these verdicts stop work, and they are usually made from a paraphrase. The scout
  you would run when work resumed costs the same now.
- Not for: ordinary difficulty estimates that do not decide whether work happens.

**The urge to estimate is the cue to scout.** When you feel "this is multi-week" or "this
must be built from scratch", treat that as the signal to search (the library, the
literature, a reduction attempt) before writing the estimate down.
{{#if modules.scans}}
`bin/scope-estimate-scan <doc>` lists the estimate phrases in a draft; for each, confirm
you scouted before you wrote it.
{{/if}}
**An inherited estimate must be tagged.** When you copy a difficulty estimate from an
older document into a new one, either cite the scout that produced it or tag it
`[unverified, inherited from <doc> <date>]`.
- Not for: dated progress logs that record what was believed at the time.

**A claim about your own runtime needs one probe.** "I can't measure X", "I have no access
to Y", "that tool isn't available" will shape the plan. Look first (list the directory,
read the settings, run the command with `--help`).
- Why: these claims are easy to make from habit and cheap to check.

**Check the known-fragile step of an inherited result first.** When you inherit a result
(from an agent, an earlier session, a draft) whose load-bearing step depends on something
you already know fails in this setting, verify that step before writing the result into
a durable document.
- Why: verifying first costs one check; writing, then discovering, then rewriting every
  copy costs a sweep.
- Not for: generic caution. Only a failure mode you have actually seen.

**Re-derive each algebraic crux yourself.** When a whole argument turns on one algebraic
condition, or on two or three (which map exists, which decomposition holds, which inclusion
is used), re-derive each by hand before building on it.
{{#if modules.gates}}
- Why: the proof referee re-derives at most three steps, of its own choosing, and only once
  the document is written; by then everything has been built on the condition.
{{else}}
- Why: a referee sees only the finished document; by then everything has been built on the
  condition.
{{/if}}
- Not for: the routine steps a referee already covers.

## Briefing another agent

**Verify a premise you hand to an agent.**{{#if modules.attempts}} (principle: verify-premise){{/if}} When a brief asserts a mathematical fact the
agent will build on (a decomposition, an identity, a structural fact), check it yourself
before dispatching.
- Why: a cold agent builds faithfully on a wrong premise, and correcting it mid-run costs
  more than the check.
- Not for: a brief that asks the agent to discover the structure.

**The exclusion list includes known trivialities and invariances.** When you send an agent
to find something new, list not only what has failed but also the known symmetries,
trivial cases and invariances of the object
{{#if modules.conjectures}}
(grep the conjecture files and their `@residue` lines for them)
{{/if}}
so it does not rediscover them and report them as new.
{{#if modules.attempts}}
The failures half comes from `bin/attempts --obstructions <target>`, one line per dead line,
not from the failed transcripts: a restarted or parallel attempt gets the problem, the
question's examples bank and that list.
{{/if}}
- Why: a fresh agent re-derives the obvious symmetry and calls it a result.

## Transcribe a literature report before citing it

A search agent's report exists only in the session. When a durable document cites it,
copy the report, with its verification tags, to a committed `lit-report-<topic>.md` beside
the citing document{{#if modules.experiments}} (in the experiment's directory when an experiment cites it){{/if}}, in the same commit that adds the citation.
- Why: otherwise the citation points at nothing once the session ends.
- The report's three tags are `[VERIFIED-FIRSTHAND]` (the exact statement was read in the
  source and quoted), `[READING]` (an interpretation, or a theorem number not quoted exactly)
  and `[PLACEMENT]` (an existence, openness or state-of-the-art claim).
{{#if modules.known_results}}
  In a `wiki/known-results/` file, `[VERIFIED-FIRSTHAND]` becomes `read: firsthand` and
  `[READING]` goes under Seeds to verify until you read the theorem yourself (`read:
  firsthand`), or two independent sources quote it exactly (`read: corroborated`).
  A `[PLACEMENT]` claim is not a stated result: cite it in prose, with its tag, and do not
  give it a known-results file.
{{/if}}
- Not for: scratch notes, which may refer to the session's report freely.

## Say "done" only after doing it

Do not tell the owner something was recorded, logged or added unless the write happened in
this turn. The `durable-ack-guard` hook catches a final message that claims a write to a
project record (by its id or file name) with no matching edit, and asks you to do it.
